@meta
id: 28-02
title: The three lines at the top
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 82
minutes: 5
vscode: nothing — the defaults are not negotiable there
open: .vimrc

@brief
Almost every vimrc you will ever read opens with the same three lines,
and most people who have them cannot tell you what they do.

    set nocompatible
    syntax on
    filetype plugin indent on

`nocompatible` turns off bug-for-bug vi emulation. It is usually
redundant: the mere existence of a vimrc already switches it off, and so
does `vim --clean`. It only matters when you source a config another way,
such as `vim -u somefile`. Harmless, conventional, first.

`syntax on` turns on highlighting. `filetype plugin indent on` is the
load-bearing one: it tells vim to detect the kind of file you opened, and
then to load that language's plugin *and* its indent rules. Without it,
`o` inside a TypeScript object does not indent itself, `gq` does not know
what a comment is, and the whole of lesson 28-13 is impossible.

Put all three at the top of your config, above the settings that are
already there.

@keys
| `O` | open a line above and start typing |
| `Enter` | in insert mode, keep going onto the next line |
| `/text` `Enter` | jump to the next match |

@hints
They go above `set number`, which is the first setting in the file.
Search for it rather than counting lines.
--
`O` opens a line *above* the cursor. Once you are in insert mode, Enter
starts the next line — you do not need to leave and re-enter for each.
--
`/set number` Enter, then `O`, then the three lines separated by Enter,
then Esc and `:wq`.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set number
set ruler
set showcmd

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

set number
set ruler
set showcmd

@solution
    /set number<Enter>  O set nocompatible<Enter> syntax on<Enter>
    filetype plugin indent on<Enter> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The trailing Enter is what leaves the blank line between the header block
and the settings. Typing the three lines and then `o` for the gap costs
the same; either is fine.

Why this order. `filetype plugin indent on` and `syntax on` both install
autocommands, and vim runs autocommands in the order they were defined —
so anything later in your config that depends on a filetype being known
has to come after these lines. Put them first and the question never
arises.

One more reason to understand these three rather than copy them: when
something in your config misbehaves, the first question is always "is
this vim's default, or did I do it?". Three lines you can explain is a
config you can debug. Five hundred lines from a gist is not.
